direction: down

title: |md
  ## Helpers vs kfuncs at the language level
| {near: top-center; shape: text}

helper: "Calling a helper" {
  style.fill: "#fef3c7"
  h_body: |md
    ```c
    #include <bpf/bpf_helpers.h>

    /* declared in header as a function pointer
       constant at a fixed enum offset:
       (void *)BPF_FUNC_map_lookup_elem */
    void *p = bpf_map_lookup_elem(&map, &key);
    ```
    - Frozen UAPI list (≈230 entries)
    - Compiles to a `call` with immediate
      operand = enum value
    - Verifier knows the proto from the
      kernel's `bpf_func_proto` struct
  |
}

kfunc: "Calling a kfunc" {
  style.fill: "#dcfce7"
  k_body: |md
    ```c
    extern struct task_struct *
    bpf_task_acquire(struct task_struct *) __ksym;

    extern void
    bpf_task_release(struct task_struct *) __ksym;

    struct task_struct *t = bpf_task_acquire(cur);
    if (!t) return 0;
    /* ... */
    bpf_task_release(t);
    ```
    - In-tree, NOT frozen UAPI
    - Compiled as ordinary symbol references
    - Resolved against kernel BTF at load time
    - Verifier checks proto from kernel BTF
    - Lifetime semantics enforced (acquire/release)
  |
}

verdict: |md
  **New BPF features arrive as kfuncs.**
  Helpers stop growing; the existing list stays frozen.
  Wherever you see `__ksym`, that's a kfunc.
| {near: bottom-center; shape: text}

helper -> verdict
kfunc -> verdict
